89
00:09:46,125 --> 00:09:51,049
[SPEAKER_06]: And the first view is from Winter Street.

90
00:09:52,732 --> 00:10:11,662
[SPEAKER_06]: Um, really kind of just want to note that the trees that were rendered here are much more mature than what we would put in, but we will, um, we're definitely taking a view as to, you know, how can we put in as large as our budget will allow and also.

91
00:10:12,483 --> 00:10:18,846
[SPEAKER_06]: Making sure that it's kind of keeping within the locus of the, of the project and not over spilling.

92
00:10:18,866 --> 00:10:22,008
[SPEAKER_06]: Um, but being able to create that shield.

93
00:10:22,505 --> 00:10:27,906
[SPEAKER_06]: The second is from Fleming Street, as well as the third.

94
00:10:27,926 --> 00:10:34,248
[SPEAKER_06]: So we're really kind of making sure that this area is very well protected.

95
00:10:34,268 --> 00:10:38,369
[SPEAKER_06]: All right, next slide, please.

96
00:10:39,670 --> 00:10:47,912
[SPEAKER_06]: And I'm gonna hand this over to John Hickok from CHA, our designer, to talk a little bit more about the lighting.

97
00:10:53,907 --> 00:10:55,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, Jen, thank you.

98
00:10:55,588 --> 00:11:06,115
[SPEAKER_02]: I appreciate the opportunity to talk about the lighting associated with the tennis courts here.

99
00:11:07,717 --> 00:11:10,739
[SPEAKER_02]: We've taken a really close look at this.

100
00:11:11,419 --> 00:11:20,686
[SPEAKER_02]: Rocco gave us kind of a design criteria when we started to make this better than what it currently is there.

101
00:11:21,486 --> 00:11:22,407
[SPEAKER_02]: As you all are aware,

102
00:11:23,559 --> 00:11:30,585
[SPEAKER_02]: Field A does have some current antiquated lighting on it that's utilized for recreational purposes.

103
00:11:31,886 --> 00:11:33,528
[SPEAKER_02]: The light poles are very low.

104
00:11:33,628 --> 00:11:41,515
[SPEAKER_02]: So the trajectory of the light allows for the spilling of the light onto the neighboring properties.

105
00:11:42,255 --> 00:11:50,923
[SPEAKER_02]: So with that, we conducted a study of the existing lights so that we could determine how we make this better for the adjoining properties.

106
00:11:51,575 --> 00:11:54,237
[SPEAKER_02]: And part of making that better is the current technology.

107
00:11:54,417 --> 00:12:00,861
[SPEAKER_02]: Current technology is LED and not metal halide as what's there currently.

108
00:12:00,901 --> 00:12:04,183
[SPEAKER_02]: And LED is a very directable light.

109
00:12:04,523 --> 00:12:07,865
[SPEAKER_02]: We can put the light on the surface with minimal spillage.

110
00:12:08,606 --> 00:12:18,932
[SPEAKER_02]: So with that being said, we created this graphic to try to show the spillage of the lights currently.

111
00:12:18,992 --> 00:12:21,174
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's that green line you see

112
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:36,888
[SPEAKER_02]: on the graphic up to the backs of the houses based on our study shows that that's approximately where we get to zero light spillage or zero foot candles for a technical term.

113
00:12:37,669 --> 00:12:44,412
[SPEAKER_02]: The other two lines that you see on there are based on our proposed lighting for the eight court.

114
00:12:45,373 --> 00:12:50,656
[SPEAKER_02]: The teal line, the outermost line is where we get to zero

115
00:12:51,272 --> 00:12:56,375
[SPEAKER_02]: foot candles or no light spillage with the proposed lighting.

116
00:12:57,015 --> 00:13:00,177
[SPEAKER_02]: The yellow line is one foot candle.

117
00:13:00,717 --> 00:13:12,543
[SPEAKER_02]: And just to put that in perspective, a parking lot at your Walmart or Target or something like that's going to be lit in the 0.25 to half a foot candle.

118
00:13:12,984 --> 00:13:15,705
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's the kind of light that we're talking about.

119
00:13:15,805 --> 00:13:17,206
[SPEAKER_02]: And zero is none.

120
00:13:17,286 --> 00:13:18,386
[SPEAKER_02]: You're back to darkness.

121
00:13:19,745 --> 00:13:27,666
[SPEAKER_02]: space there between the yellow and the teal line, it's very, very minimal light in that area.

122
00:13:29,227 --> 00:13:43,189
[SPEAKER_02]: And furthermore, the lighting system with the technologies of LED and everybody needs control of everything on their mobile devices these days, these lights are highly controllable and highly scheduled.

123
00:13:43,829 --> 00:13:47,310
[SPEAKER_02]: So these can be scheduled to turn on, turn off at certain times,

124
00:13:47,773 --> 00:14:04,605
[SPEAKER_02]: They can also be turned on and turned off through your mobile device with the appropriate app that allows tons of flexibility for the athletic department, campus safety, and the such, so that these lights can be monitored and turned on and off as appropriate.

125
00:14:04,985 --> 00:14:17,414
[SPEAKER_02]: We just wanted to show tonight the criteria that we were up against in putting this lighting design together, as well as how this has benefited

126
00:14:17,780 --> 00:14:29,207
[SPEAKER_02]: benefit of the community and by removing the lights on on field day and putting in new lights on the tennis court to to minimize that that spillage to the community thanks john.

127
00:14:35,031 --> 00:14:41,335
[SPEAKER_06]: All right, so we'll return to kind of wrap up and give a little bit more on.

128
00:14:42,046 --> 00:14:43,287
[SPEAKER_06]: of a synopsis here.

129
00:14:43,747 --> 00:15:02,719
[SPEAKER_06]: So purpose and benefits, the proposed location, you know, will provide for a better athlete support with the proximity of the Tisch Center in addition to any community members wanting to utilize the courts and being able to use Cousins Gym as a touchdown point as well.

130
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:11,365
[SPEAKER_06]: Existing court location can be used for housing and that keeps students up on the upper campus area

131
00:15:12,856 --> 00:15:24,679
[SPEAKER_06]: located there to Rocco's Point near academic buildings and dining halls and the lake.

132
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:33,341
[SPEAKER_06]: This program will be continued community use when not used by athletics.

133
00:15:34,942 --> 00:15:40,563
[SPEAKER_06]: As I mentioned before, when I talked about the construction, it's a concrete acrylic surface

134
00:15:41,408 --> 00:15:52,314
[SPEAKER_06]: It has superior crack resistance to allow for consistent play and allow for, you know, less wear and tear over time.

135
00:15:53,094 --> 00:16:05,040
[SPEAKER_06]: All improvements to the court in the spectator seating and the areas surrounding will all meet or exceed the handicap accessibility requirement.

136
00:16:06,448 --> 00:16:28,405
[SPEAKER_06]: As John noted, the state-of-the-art LED sports lighting will allow for sharp cutoff, minimizing the light spill, and improving the conditions that we currently have, which the lights will also help increase the opportunity for play by both the Tufts athletic community and the outside Medford community.

137
00:16:28,726 --> 00:16:35,151
[SPEAKER_06]: And in addition, it also meets academic needs and schedule of Tufts students, allowing for them to practice

138
00:16:36,257 --> 00:16:39,881
[SPEAKER_06]: after dark, but also being able to keep their academic schedule intact.

139
00:16:42,463 --> 00:16:42,964
[SPEAKER_06]: Next slide.

140
00:16:44,946 --> 00:16:59,940
[SPEAKER_06]: So the next steps we have, just to kind of outline moving forward from our team, we have our community meeting presently, and we would move forward to apply for our building permit.

141
00:17:00,583 --> 00:17:10,069
[SPEAKER_06]: The anticipated schedule is to begin construction in fall 2022 with earthwork, concrete installation, and fencing.

142
00:17:10,089 --> 00:17:29,182
[SPEAKER_06]: And then in the spring or late spring, early summer, we would do final court construction with the court surfacing, spectator circulation, lighting, landscaping, and just final completion of the project for folks to enjoy.

